---
import Document from "../components/Document.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, GITHUB_REPOSITORY_URL, LICENSE_URL, T3_SITE_URL } from "../lib/site";
---

<Document
  title="Legal"
  description="J5 Code's license, its relationship to T3 Code, and whose terms apply to the services it connects to."
  updated="2026-10-04"
>
  <p>
    J5 Code is open-source software maintained by Jackson Miller. It is free, and its source is at{" "}
    <a href={GITHUB_REPOSITORY_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">github.com/Jacksondr5/j5code</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>License</h2>
  <p>
    J5 Code is released under the{" "}
    <a href={LICENSE_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MIT License</a>. The license
    says what you may do with the software, and that it comes without warranty.
  </p>

  <h2>T3 Code</h2>
  <p>
    J5 Code is a fork of <a href={T3_SITE_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">T3 Code</a>,
    which is made by T3 Tools Inc. and also MIT licensed. J5 Code is an independent project: T3
    Tools does not make, endorse, or support it.
  </p>
  <p>
    T3 Connect and T3 Account are T3 Tools' hosted services. J5 Code does not run them, and J5 Code
    releases are not set up to use them.
  </p>

  <h2>Services you connect</h2>
  <p>
    J5 Code runs coding agents through providers you choose and sign in to yourself, such as
    OpenAI, Anthropic, and Cursor. Your use of each provider is under that provider's terms and
    privacy policy, not J5 Code's.
  </p>

  <h2>Privacy</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/privacy-policy">privacy page</a> says what J5 Code sends from your machines and
    how to turn it off.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p><a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a></p>
</Document>
